{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778979260761,"sessionID":"ses_1cc94d5f8ffeT7HuRxhCKpOV7c","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_tJCbtZMRLnBKHLzUAlWBflqj","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/docs/relations_guide.md","offset":213,"limit":70},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/docs/relations_guide.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n213: ## Recording general cellular characteristics\n214: \n215: The ontology [<u>PATO</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato),\n216: has a rich set of terms that can be used to record the general\n217: characteristics of cells, such as their morphology. These are recorded\n218: using [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053).\n219: \n220: In choosing PATO terms, avoid those referring to some change in\n221: characteristic (e.g,.’ increased branchiness’). The following list of\n222: examples is not exhaustive:\n223: \n224: ### Recording Morphology\n225: \n226: PATO has a set of general morphology terms which may be applicable to\n227: cells.\n228: \n229: For example, [erythrocyte](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000765)\n230: subClassOf [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053)\n231: *some* [biconcave](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002039)\n232: \n233: PATO also has a set of terms for [<u>specific cell morphologies</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato) (mostly\n234: neuronal), e.g.\n235: \n236: ‘Betz cell’ subClassOf ‘has characteristic’ some ‘standard pyramidal morphology’\n237: \n238: \n239: ### Recording cell shape\n240: \n241: Cell shape is an important characteristic for classifying certain cell types, particularly epithelial cells. Use the [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) relation with appropriate PATO shape terms.\n242: \n243: For flattened (squamous) cells:\n244: \n245: ['squamous epithelial cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000076) equivalentTo ['epithelial cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000066) and ([**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) *some* [flattened](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002254))\n246: \n247: For cuboidal cells:\n248: \n249: ['cuboidal epithelial cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_9900001) equivalentTo ['epithelial cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000066) and ([**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) *some* [cuboidal](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002312))\n250: \n251: ### Recording nuclear number \n252: \n253: To record the number of nuclei in a cell, use a PATO subclass\n254: under the term ['nucleate quality'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001404) with the ['has\n255: characteristic'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) relation.\n256: \n257: ![image](https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/assets/112839/4b6aa141-c42f-4bff-9d9e-bdb71a17e882)\n258: \n259: For example, \n260: \n261: [platelet](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000233) subClassOf (['has_characteristic'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) *some* [**anucleate**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001405))\n262: \n263: Note - that PATO includes bridging axioms that infer part relationships\n264: based on these characteristics.\n265: \n266: e.g.\n267: \n268: cell and ('has characteristic' some multinucleate) SubClassOf 'has part'\n269: some nucleus\n270:                        \n271: ## Taxon constraints\n272: \n273: See\n274: <https://oboacademy.github.io/obook/explanation/taxon-constraints-explainer/>.\n275: \n\n(End of file - total 275 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## Recording general cellular characteristics\n\nThe ontology [<u>PATO</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato),\nhas a rich set of terms that can be used to record the general\ncharacteristics of cells, such as their morphology. These are recorded\nusing [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053).\n\nIn choosing PATO terms, avoid those referring to some change in\ncharacteristic (e.g,.’ increased branchiness’). The following list of\nexamples is not exhaustive:\n\n### Recording Morphology\n\nPATO has a set of general morphology terms which may be applicable to\ncells.\n\nFor example, [erythrocyte](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000765)\nsubClassOf [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053)\n*some* [biconcave](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002039)\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__pr_result__/docs/relations_guide.md","time":{"start":1778979260725,"end":1778979260755}},"id":"prt_e336d4920001hF0s4uDjP5EWTO","sessionID":"ses_1cc94d5f8ffeT7HuRxhCKpOV7c","messageID":"msg_e336d294f001auW4mE5XcF0GN6"}}
